## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Simplify and consolidate Tool Shed before adding the first-class 100k Project Executive View.
Preserve Tool Shed's useful behavior while reducing maintained concepts, duplicated execution
paths, public surface area, installed footprint, and code that implements the same underlying
authority, lifecycle, reconciliation, projection, or compatibility behavior more than once.

This Idea owns the architectural prerequisite. The companion `IDEA-0028` owns the 100k operator
capability and must be re-reviewed against the architecture settled here.

### Why It Matters

Tool Shed has grown through successful additions, but each independently correct feature adds
maintenance, validation, documentation, migration, and operator-understanding cost. A current
repository inspection found 86 top-level Python scripts with about 57,000 lines, roughly 27,000
test lines, several core modules between 1,500 and 2,700 lines, and a broad command surface.

The 100k view could otherwise become another authority, lifecycle, renderer, report, and command
family. Consolidating first creates a stable seam so 100k can replace overlapping surfaces rather
than merely joining them.

### Desired Outcome

Tool Shed retains its observable capabilities and safety boundaries through a smaller coherent
architecture with fewer independently maintained concepts and paths. One evidence-backed
capability disposition records what is kept, merged, moved, replaced, or retired. Everyday project
runtime, optional integrations, upgrade/recovery machinery, and maintainer-only qualification are
separated where that reduces installed and cognitive weight without removing functionality.

One shared project projection and lifecycle/closure foundation is available for existing status,
overview, dashboard, and future 100k consumers. The relevant foundation is implemented before
100k coding begins, while unrelated cleanup may continue independently.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Preserve current authority, identity, stale-write, recovery, recursive-closure, evidence, privacy,
  cross-platform, and provider-portability guarantees unless an explicit superseding decision is
  accepted and qualified.
- Preserve useful functionality; reduction must not be achieved by silently dropping behavior or
  weakening validation.
- Prefer incremental convergence and deletion of replaced paths over a high-risk rewrite.
- Measure reduction in maintained concepts, public routes, production paths, installed footprint,
  cross-module coupling, and validation cost; raw line count alone is not the objective.
- Keep tests that protect behavior. Consolidate test boilerplate only when coverage and failure
  attribution remain at least as strong.
- Do not design or implement the 100k feature here beyond establishing the reusable architectural
  seam and explicit dependency.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| One authority/outcome/closure kernel | Removes competing interpretations and gives every surface the same truth | Broad refactoring could disturb mature safety rules | Explore incrementally from existing document, relationship, cycle, and closure services |
| One shared project projection | Lets overview, status, dashboard, and 100k consume one model | Projection must preserve privacy and legacy semantics | Strong candidate and direct prerequisite for IDEA-0028 |
| Runtime/integration/maintainer distribution layers | Shrinks ordinary installed snapshots while retaining canonical functionality | Packaging and updater compatibility need explicit proof | Inventory what clients actually require before selecting boundaries |
| Declarative command registry and generated reference | Reduces help, routing, and documentation drift | Migration could affect aliases and provider adapters | Compare current routes and parser registrations before design |
| Defined compatibility retirement policy | Stops legacy branches from accumulating forever | Older workspaces need a supported migration path | Keep converters available without keeping every legacy path in everyday runtime |
| Strangler-style consolidation | Delivers bounded reductions with parity evidence | Temporary duplication exists during migration | Prefer over a whole-system rewrite |

## Open Questions

- Which Tool Shed capabilities are essential core behavior, optional integration behavior,
  maintainer behavior, or historical compatibility?
- Which scripts and commands are distinct capabilities, and which are duplicate mechanisms or
  aliases over the same concept?
- What single project projection can serve local overview, status, dashboard reporting, and 100k?
- Which project map, focus-area, index, overview, and navigation responsibilities should be merged
  or narrowed when 100k is introduced?
- Which compatibility versions and paths still have real consumers, and what evidence permits
  isolation or retirement?
- What reduction measures and parity gates are strong enough to prevent cosmetic simplification?
- How much of the maintainer qualification and release surface must ship inside every disconnected
  project snapshot?

## Decisions

- Keep consolidation and the 100k Executive View as separate Idea Briefs with an explicit
  dependency.
- Settle the consolidation architecture before finalizing the 100k design.
- Implement the shared projection and lifecycle foundation needed by 100k before coding the 100k
  feature itself; unrelated reductions need not block later 100k discovery.
- Require 100k to retire or consolidate overlapping surfaces rather than ship as an additional
  parallel planning system.
- Preserve capabilities through consolidation; do not use reduced test coverage as a size metric.

